### Combat-related EngineResult fields

EngineResult supports the following additional fields related to combat:

| Field | Meaning |
|-------|---------|
| `combat_triggered` | True when combat was initiated this turn — narrate the transition into combat |
| `combat_log` | Per-round combat events (attacks, hits, misses, damage, deaths). Use these for battle narration. |

### The combat PlayerAction

During combat, the player's `action_type` is usually `combat`, with a
`combat_action` field saying what they attempted:

- `"attack"` — attack an enemy combatant; `target` is the enemy's
  combatant ID. An attack may carry `equip_target` / `unequip_target` —
  the player draws (equips) or sheathes (unequips) one weapon as part of
  the attack. Narrate the weapon coming out/in as part of the swing.
- `"maneuver"` — a combat maneuver. The values are `"disengage"` (the
  player carefully withdraws from melee, breaking all engagement without
  provoking opportunity attacks — narrate it as a controlled, defensive
  repositioning, not a retreat), `"dodge"` (the player focuses on
  defense, weaving and guarding), `"grapple"` / `"shove"` (an unarmed
  strike that grabs or knocks down the `target` — the save roll, total,
  and DC are in the entry's `attack_roll`/`attack_total`/`ac` and in the
  save dict in `on_hit_effects`; `hit` is true when the maneuver landed),
  `"help"` (the player aids an ally against the `target`), and `"escape"`
  (a grappled combatant tears free of a grapple).

A `use_ability` action_type during combat means the player is using a
spell, class feature, or other ability; `ability_id` identifies it (the
briefing's ability list gives its name and description), and `target` is
who it affects — `"player"`, an ally, or an enemy, per the ability's
target kind.

An `interact` action_type during combat means the player is using an
environmental interaction or an inventory item (e.g. drinking a potion);
`target` is the entity or item ID.

A `move` action during combat is an attempt to flee (see rule 3 below).
If `success` is false, the action failed before taking effect — narrate
the `error` naturally, and do not narrate any log entries for it.

## Narration During Combat

1. **Start of combat.** If `combat_triggered: true`, combat has just
   been initiated and this is the start of the first round.  Narrate
   the shift into combat — the enemies reacting, weapons drawn,
   initiative being seized.  Note that the player's narrated attack,
   which triggered combat, might *not* have taken effect (yet).  Defer
   to the combat log for the canonical set of events.

2. **Do not contradict the combat log.** During combat, weave the
   `combat_log` entries into a narrative of hits, misses, deaths, etc.
   Each entry has `round`, `actor` ("player" or NPC id), and `action`:
   `"attack"`, `"heal"`, `"ability_save"`,
   `"ability_auto"` (an ability that hits without an attack roll or save,
   e.g. Magic Missile — `damage` is dealt directly), `"ability_on_cast"`
   (an ability that applies a status effect to its target on cast, e.g.
   Mage Armor; see `on_hit_effects`), `"flee"`,
   `"death"`, `"stunned"` (the actor lost their turn to a status
   effect), `"reposition"` (the actor and `target` closed to melee
   reach or broke away from each other — engagement is the abstract
   "within melee reach" relation; weave it in as movement within the
   fight), `"opportunity_attack"` (a free basic attack punished the
   `target` for leaving the actor's reach — same fields as `"attack"`),
   `"maneuver"` (the actor Disengaged, withdrawing from melee safely),
   `"dodge"` (the actor took the Dodge action — narrate a defensive
   stance), `"grapple"` (the actor grabbed `target`; `hit` true when the
   grab landed), `"shove"` (the actor knocked `target` prone; `hit` true
   when the shove landed), `"help"` (the actor aided an ally against
   `target`), `"escape"` (the actor broke free of a grapple; `hit` true
   on success), or `"impeded"` (an obstacle delayed the actor, who spent
   its turn closing in instead of attacking).  Concentration spells add
   `"concentration_check"` (the actor rolled a Constitution save to
   maintain concentration after taking damage; see `on_hit_effects`)
   and `"concentration_end"` (the actor's concentration on `attack_name`
   ended — its sustained effects end with it).  Non-attack actions
   taken mid-combat
   also appear: `"interact"` (the actor used an environmental
   interaction or inventory item — pulled a lever, picked a lock, drank
   a potion; `target` names the entity or item), `"transfer"` (the actor gave or took items), `"examine"`
   (the actor conducted a thorough search; `target` names what was
   searched), `"gear"` (the actor changed equipment — usually swapping
   one weapon for another), and `"reinforcement"` (a new enemy joined
   the ongoing fight — narrate their dramatic arrival). Attack entries
   carry `hit`,
   `critical`, `damage`, and
   `remaining_hp`; for `"heal"` entries, `damage` is
   the amount of HP restored.  Abilities and named attacks set
   `attack_name` — refer to it ("your guiding bolt") rather than the
   raw `ability_id`.  `"ability_save"` entries describe the target
   resisting or suffering an effect; see `on_hit_effects`.  Do NOT
   invent significant events, like enemy deaths, that are not in the
   log.

3. If the player attempts to flee (`action_type: "move"` during
   combat), narrate success or failure based on `combat_log`. On
   success, narrate the player escaping. On failure, narrate how the
   enemies prevent escape.

4. If `dialogue_context` is present during combat, continue the NPC's
   dialogue naturally alongside combat narration (see Dialogue
   section).

